/* Wealth Lab Module 16 · Financial Adviser Practice & Comprehensive Capstone · 2026 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM16Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m16-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","taxYear":2026,"lessonIds":["L16.S1","L16.S2","L16.S3","L16.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L16.S1","title":"Fiduciary Duty and Conflicts","subtitle":"Professional standards, informed consent, product incentives, fee drag, client privacy and recommendation records","prerequisites":["L11.S2 marginal tax and return comparisons","L12.S2 retirement account options","L13.S1 protection needs","L15.S4 opportunity costs and asset selection"],"objectives":["Distinguish the SEC investment-adviser fiduciary standard, broker-dealer Regulation Best Interest and CFP Board fiduciary duty","Explain care, loyalty, conflicts, scope, monitoring and documentation within each applicable standard","Evaluate Form ADV Part 2, Form CRS and product disclosures as different materials","Calculate annual layered investment fees and compounded opportunity cost","Detect sales conflicts, proprietary-product incentives, rollover conflicts and misleading performance presentations","Construct a professional client-first recommendation memo with reasonable alternatives","Recognize confidentiality, client informed consent and appropriate professional referral boundaries"],"sections":[{"title":"Fiduciary duty is a legal and professional relationship, not a job title","body":["The US investment adviser fiduciary standard, interpreted by the Securities and Exchange Commission under the Investment Advisers Act, entails a duty of care and a duty of loyalty in the scope of the advisory relationship. Advisers generally must provide advice in clients' best interests and make full and fair disclosure of material conflicts such that clients can give informed consent; when a conflict cannot be adequately addressed, a professional may need to eliminate it. The duty is not satisfied simply by calling oneself a fiduciary on a website.","Broker-dealers giving covered recommendations to retail customers face Regulation Best Interest, which has disclosure, care, conflicts-of-interest and compliance obligations. A dual-registered professional may act in different capacities on different occasions, and the client deserves to know which standard applies to the particular recommendation. CFP® professionals separately owe CFP Board's fiduciary duty whenever providing financial advice to a client, subject to the body's professional standards.","Ethical analysis begins by identifying the adviser, firm, engagement scope, compensation, legal capacity, product recommendations and documents actually provided. Avoid treating the phrase 'financial adviser' as proof of registration, certification or unconditional future monitoring. Wealth Lab is financial education and does not certify or license students to provide regulated advice."],"worked":["SEC investment adviser: statutory fiduciary duty of care and loyalty as applicable.","Retail broker-dealer securities recommendation: SEC Regulation Best Interest and its four components.","CFP® professional financial advice: CFP Board fiduciary duty and professional practice standards.","The same individual can have distinct roles; confirm the capacity and agreement at each recommendation."],"practice":[["Does advertising the word 'fiduciary' establish a professional's registration or exact contractual responsibilities?","No. Confirm registration, professional credentials, engagement documents, actual capacity and conflicts in official records."],["Are Reg BI and the investment-adviser fiduciary standard identical in every detail?","No. They are different regulatory frameworks with distinct application triggers and obligations, even though both address acting in clients' best interests."]]},{"title":"Client-first standards demand a reproducible recommendation process","body":["A reasonable recommendation requires understanding a client's actual goals, assets, income, obligations, risk tolerance, risk capacity, liquidity horizon, tax position, financial knowledge and available alternatives. Risk tolerance describes willingness to experience uncertainty; risk capacity describes the household's actual financial ability to withstand loss. A client may love aggressive stocks and still lack the capacity to risk a first-home closing reserve. The professional must distinguish preference from feasibility.","Under the SEC interpretation and related account recommendations guidance, investment advisers have care obligations that may include understanding a client's investment profile, cost, account services and reasonably available alternatives as appropriate to the engagement. Broker-dealers have specific Reg BI requirements for recommendations in their scope. An investment professional should compare an ongoing advisory account with an available brokerage solution when the costs and desired services materially differ.","Document the facts used and the limits of the work, rather than filling missing information with favorable estimates. If the client's life insurance, trust deed or taxable retirement basis cannot be verified, mark those findings unresolved and defer a product-specific conclusion. Fiduciary-quality conduct can mean recommending no sale, no rollover or no new investment."],"worked":["Client holds $10,000 required for closing in 10 months and $5,000 accessible savings.","A proposal to place all $10,000 in high-volatility equity must confront the 10-month fixed cash need.","A 25% market decline would reduce that earmarked investment to $7,500, creating a $2,500 closing shortfall.","The risk tolerance answer 'comfortable with stocks' does not resolve the inability to fund the closing."],"practice":[["Why does a high willingness to invest aggressively not prove high risk capacity?","The client may lack other cash for fixed debts, housing or medical obligations and cannot financially withstand a loss."],["Can the best recommendation be to avoid buying a product?","Yes. If liquidity, suitability or costs fail a reasonable alternative analysis, a prudent conclusion may be to retain suitable existing accounts or cash."]]},{"title":"Conflicts: identify, disclose, mitigate, or decline","body":["Financial incentives can bias advice. Product commissions, trail fees, revenue sharing, managed-account asset fees, proprietary funds, referral relationships and bonus targets can affect which strategy is presented. Fee-only descriptions do not automatically eliminate asset-based incentives, such as discouraging a client from using investments to pay down an expensive mortgage. Material conflicts must be understood in the actual account and service context, not as an abstract warning.","For SEC investment advisers, sufficiently specific material-conflict disclosure and informed consent are critical; a boilerplate statement that a conflict 'may' exist can be inadequate when it actually exists. Broker-dealer Reg BI also includes firm-level conflict-mitigation and certain conflict-elimination requirements, not just disclosure. CFP Board duties likewise require managing material conflicts and acting in the client's interest. The applicable legal requirements depend on the role and facts.","An evaluation file should disclose the adviser is compensated $2,000 annually, what happens when assets leave the account, whether the recommendation earns extra commission, why lower-cost alternatives were rejected and what protections remain. An adviser who cannot explain these facts should not be allowed to substitute testimonials or attractive projected returns for evidence."],"worked":["Illustrative adviser receives 1% annually on $200,000 = $2,000 first-year advisory fee.","Recommended fund expense 0.50% adds $1,000 on same opening balance.","Combined opening-year explicit percentages 1.50% or $3,000 before other fees and performance.","An adviser paid on managed assets may have an incentive about keeping money invested versus debt payoff."],"practice":[["Does simply listing a commission in paperwork always cure an unsuitable recommendation?","No. Disclosure alone does not erase care, best-interest or conflict-mitigation obligations under the applicable standard."],["Why can an AUM fee create a conflict even when no product commission is paid?","The professional may be financially rewarded for retaining managed assets instead of recommending suitable cash use, debt payment or a lower-cost account."]]},{"title":"Fees compound through time and affect the after-fee portfolio","body":["Fee comparisons must use the same investment assumptions, starting balance, services and period. An advisory charge of 1% and fund expense ratio of 0.5% initially sum to 1.5% of a portfolio if assessed on comparable balances; real billing conventions and market values differ. A percentage fee reduces the capital remaining to compound every year, so the cost is not merely the sum of first-year invoices.","Suppose $200,000 earns hypothetical 6% gross annually for twenty years with no deposits or withdrawals. A zero-fee illustrative ending value is $200,000×1.06^20. Modeling a simplified 1.5-percentage-point net reduction gives $200,000×1.045^20. Their difference represents the compounding opportunity cost of this stylized assumption, not an adviser-specific prediction. Precise fee timing requires calculating gross growth and charges separately.","Compare the fee against real services: tax-aware planning, retirement analysis, estate coordination and behavioral support may provide value for some clients, but cannot be assumed valuable without knowing the household's needs. Seek the all-in cost including transaction spreads, account charges, underlying funds and advisory layers. State whether fees are fixed, hourly, subscription, AUM-based or commission-based."],"worked":["Initial portfolio $200,000, hypothetical gross 6% annual return for 20 years.","Illustrative no-fee value $200,000×1.06^20≈$641,427.","Illustrative simplified 4.5% net rate value $200,000×1.045^20≈$482,327.","Illustrative 20-year terminal gap ≈$159,100, highly assumption-sensitive and not a market forecast."],"practice":[["Why is a 1.5% annual layered fee not simply a $3,000 one-time lifetime cost?","Ongoing fees reduce assets each period and the investment growth those assets could have earned; future balances and actual billing affect the cost."],["Does selecting the cheapest adviser automatically guarantee the best advice?","No. A reasonable comparison weighs scope, quality, client objectives, risk, access, alternatives and total fees rather than price alone."]]},{"title":"A rollover is a decision about protections, service and costs","body":["Moving workplace retirement assets to an IRA can change investment expenses, account services, distribution flexibility, creditor protections, access to certain investments and required minimum distribution treatment. A rollover may generate new compensation for an adviser; that creates an incentive that must be examined. A prudent analysis includes keeping the current plan, transfer to another employer plan when eligible, rollover to IRA, and any permitted distribution with tax consequences.","Suppose a workplace plan offers a diversified low-fee option at 0.15% of assets, while the proposed IRA advisory arrangement costs 1.00% plus funds at 0.30%. On $100,000 the stylized annual starting cost is $150 in plan versus $1,300 in IRA, a $1,150 first-year difference before transaction fees. The IRA may offer additional planning features, but the adviser must identify and justify the value to this client rather than asserting portability is automatically superior.","Check actual plan documents, expense disclosures, special employer-stock rules, distribution eligibility, rollover mechanics, tax withholding and potential pro-rata conversion implications. The plan's fiduciary and the personal adviser may operate under different regimes. Never promise a tax-free rollover without verifying direct transfer procedures and the account's tax character."],"worked":["Workplace-plan fees 0.15%×$100,000=$150/year opening cost.","Proposed IRA adviser 1.0% plus fund 0.3% =1.3%×$100,000=$1,300.","Opening annual cost difference $1,150.","Account choice requires service, creditor protection, investment choices and tax analysis, not fee alone."],"practice":[["Why does an adviser have a possible incentive to recommend an IRA rollover?","Receiving management authority over rollover assets may increase compensation, making objective alternatives and conflict disclosure essential."],["Can a plan-to-IRA rollover always be evaluated solely on portfolio expense ratios?","No. Distribution rights, taxes, creditor protections, employer stock, services, investment choices and individual goals also matter."]]},{"title":"Confidentiality and the limits of documentation","body":["Financial planning requires sensitive information: household income, debts, insurance beneficiaries, estate documents, marital status, disabilities and minors' needs. An adviser should collect only information necessary for the defined scope, protect access, document authorization and avoid revealing records to unrelated parties. CFP Board's duties include confidentiality and client privacy; registered firms have additional applicable privacy, cybersecurity and records obligations.","An educational planning app storing user notes locally should communicate whether notes are synced or retained, and must never claim to have verified actual financial records without reading them. A human professional's recordkeeping, consent and secure storage duties may differ from the practices of a self-study app. Data-minimizing practice avoids collecting Social Security numbers and account credentials merely for a classroom hypothetical.","A recommendation memo should trace the dated source, assumption, client confirmation, professional calculation and proposed action. Instead of storing client secrets in unsecured text, refer to encrypted or appropriately protected records and document who has access. Family members or a future heir should only be included in private discussions with applicable authorization."],"worked":["A hypothetical client presents $10,000 cash, $5,000 debts and a private minor-beneficiary record.","Analysis can retain broad numbers without storing complete bank account numbers or minors' identifiers.","Verification status 'confirmed from statement' differs from 'client-estimated'.","If ownership or beneficiary facts are unverified, defer estate conclusions rather than disclose or invent private records."],"practice":[["Why should an adviser avoid asking for bank login credentials in a planning intake form?","Credentials are unnecessary for many planning calculations and create preventable access, privacy and cybersecurity risks."],["Why must a planning memo distinguish verified records from client estimates?","Recommendations based on unverified asset values or beneficiaries can fail when actual contracts, loans or records differ."]]},{"title":"Ethical case: unsuitable permanent insurance sales pitch","body":["Imagine a household with a $7,000 high-rate credit-card balance, a depleted emergency fund and only $350 monthly reliable surplus. A seller recommends a new permanent life policy requiring $300 monthly solely to borrow for a first-home down payment. The seller earns a substantial commission and compares only a favorable non-guaranteed cash-value illustration. This raises questions about liquidity suitability, product expense, contract values and compensation conflicts.","An appropriate analysis begins with affordable death-benefit needs, emergency expenses, the card's 22% APR, eligibility for low-cost term protection and how long the intended home purchase is away. If allocating $300 to premium leaves $50 monthly for reserves, a new policy may materially postpone the homeowner's goal. A tax-advantaged policy loan still creates debt, loan interest and possible lapse risk; placing the contract in an irrevocable trust may further restrict personal access.","The professional memo must show the complete comparison, the agent's conflicts and an alternative with documented downside. It should not suggest a trust, LLC or insurance wrapper automatically eliminates tax, increases net worth or guarantees a mortgage down payment. A client-centered recommendation can explain that some permanent policies serve valid long-term protection or estate purposes while finding this particular transaction unsupported."],"worked":["Monthly reliable surplus $350, proposed permanent premium $300.","Separate housing/emergency saving left $50 monthly, only $600/year before returns.","Card $7,000 at hypothetical 22% APR incurs $1,540 annual simple-cost illustration if balance unchanged.","Commission incentive and lack of guaranteed loan evidence are material review issues."],"practice":[["Does a salesperson's non-guaranteed illustration establish future policy-loan availability for a home purchase?","No. Contract guarantees, charges, owner rights, underwriting and lender rules must be verified separately."],["What makes a conflict review more than accusing a salesperson of bad intent?","It identifies economic incentives, actual disclosures, specific client risks and reasonably available alternatives with documented financial consequences."]]},{"title":"Build and defend a client-first recommendation record","body":["Finish with a formal written analysis defining the engagement, professional capacity, client objectives, verified data, assumptions, options, fee comparisons, downside cases, conflicts, recommendation, referral needs and monitoring responsibility. Distinguish essential hard constraints from flexible preferences. The client should be able to explain what will happen in adverse conditions, what the professional will do, and what the client retains responsibility for implementing.","An analyst can score two hypothetical strategies on cost, liquidity and risk, but a recommendation must not be forced by a single weighted total if a mandatory constraint fails. A household that requires its $10,000 closing money next month should not accept an illiquid product only because the product scores well on estimated long-term returns. Define who will verify regulatory licenses, product guarantees, mortgage funding and tax consequences before execution.","Quality assurance involves an independent reviewer re-running all important calculations and checking citations. A correction is documented rather than concealed. Wealth Lab's numerical labs are intended to train that habit; the course remains independent educational material and does not create a regulated advisory relationship or professional credential."],"worked":["Strategy A 1.5% annual layered fee on $200,000 → $3,000 opening cost.","Strategy B 0.15% plan expense on $200,000 → $300 opening cost.","Opening cost difference $2,700, before services, eligibility and future balance differences.","Recommend based on client's facts and service value, not merely a cost minimization metric."],"practice":[["What belongs in an auditable recommendation record?","Client goals, facts and verification, alternatives, full costs, conflicts, uncertainty, adverse cases, responsible implementation and follow-up."],["What should a student conclude when a critical insurance, trust or account document is unavailable?","Identify the unknown and seek appropriate verification rather than presenting an unsupported specific recommendation as certain."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC 2019 investment adviser fiduciary standard interpretation","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2019-07-12/html/2019-12208.htm"],["SEC Regulation Best Interest official compliance guide","https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/small-business-compliance-guides/regulation-best-interest"],["SEC Form ADV conflicts related to adviser compensation","https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/division-investment-management-frequently-asked-questions/frequently-asked-questions-regarding-disclosure-certain-financial-conflicts-related-investment"],["CFP Board Code of Ethics and Standards of Conduct","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/code-of-ethics-and-standards-of-conduct"],["SEC standards of conduct account recommendation bulletin","https://www.sec.gov/about/divisions-offices/division-trading-markets/broker-dealers/staff-bulletin-standards-conduct-broker-dealers-investment-advisers-account-recommendations-retail"],["SEC Investor.gov investment professional IAPD disclosure","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/using-iapd"],["IRS retirement plan rollover overview","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-beneficiary"],["SEC Form CRS official FAQ","https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/trading-markets-frequently-asked-questions/frequently-asked-questions-form-crs"]],"assessment":{"title":"Fiduciary best-interest and conflict-management file","prompt":"For a client with $200k portfolio, compute a 1% adviser plus 0.5% fund layered initial cost and simplified 20-year compounding difference relative to no fees at 6% gross. Compare $100k low-cost employer plan 0.15% versus proposed IRA 1.3%; identify service, tax and creditor-protection tradeoffs. Write SEC adviser/Reg BI/CFP professional scope and conflict distinctions without conflating them. Analyze a $7k 22% card with $350 surplus and a $300 permanent-policy premium pitch. Audit Form ADV, Form CRS, official registration and client data handling. Produce a client-best-interest memo with alternatives and referrals.","rubric":["Correct regulatory frameworks and role delineation (25%)","Transparent complete fee and rollover analysis (20%)","Client protection, liquidity and conflicts (25%)","Privacy and verified supporting documentation (15%)","Professional best-interest alternatives and memo clarity (15%)"],"answerKey":["Opening fees 200k×(1%+.5%)=$3k; simplified gross 6%/net 4.5% 20-year compounding shows material difference.","On $100k, plan 0.15%=$150 versus IRA 1.3%=$1,300; first-year difference $1,150 before other costs.","Securities advisers, broker-dealers and CFP credential holders operate under distinguishable overlapping but nonidentical standards.","$350 surplus−$300 monthly premium leaves $50, or $600 annual cash for emergency and home needs.","Reg BI requires care, disclosure, conflict and compliance obligations; the adviser's fiduciary duties include care and loyalty; CFP professional fiduciary applies to financial advice under CFP Board standards.","Registration, conflicts and discipline should be verified using official IAPD, BrokerCheck and Connecticut records."]}},{"id":"L16.S2","title":"The Financial Planning Process","subtitle":"Structured discovery, risk analysis, alternatives, implementation responsibility, and monitoring over the client lifecycle","prerequisites":["L2 income, bills and spending plans","L6 borrowing, mortgage affordability and liquidity","L10 investment diversification","L13 family protection and estate continuity","L16.S1 professional conduct and conflicts"],"objectives":["Apply CFP Board's seven-step financial planning process without implying professional certification","Create a full data-discovery and client authorization inventory","Distinguish goals, values, risk tolerance, risk capacity and immediate obligations","Construct household balance sheet, cash flow, reserve, debt, home and retirement analyses","Compare status quo with alternative integrated plans","Assign accountable implementation, referral and monitoring tasks","Document data quality and revised planning assumptions"],"sections":[{"title":"The seven-step planning cycle is a decision architecture","body":["CFP Board's financial planning process includes understanding personal and financial circumstances, identifying and selecting goals, analyzing existing and potential alternative courses of action, developing recommendations, presenting recommendations, implementing agreed recommendations, and monitoring progress and updating. In educational practice these seven steps prevent product selection from outrunning actual client understanding. They also make each later decision revisitable as income, law and family needs change.","The planner's engagement should identify services and exclusions in writing as appropriate under the applicable professional standard. A calculation performed to explain mortgage affordability does not automatically mean the professional will prepare a tax return, draft a trust or monitor investments every month. A client's agreement to gather data and implement choices is essential, but an adviser must not push responsibilities onto the client that the professional has explicitly assumed.","During training, every module can be connected to a stage of this cycle: first gather source statements, quantify the current position, evaluate tradeoffs, present alternatives, sequence actions, and establish measurable review triggers. The process is not a seal of accreditation for Wealth Lab or a license to advise others."],"worked":["Seven planning stages: understand, goals, analyze, develop, present, implement, monitor/update.","A plan's date, scope, verified facts and responsible parties must be recorded.","Product selection before discovery skips required reasoning, regardless of product quality.","A signed plan with no implementation owner is not the same as completed financial progress."],"practice":[["Why is a comprehensive financial plan iterative rather than a one-time investment recommendation?","Life, laws, market values, employment and client goals change; effective planning includes implementation checks and ongoing updates within agreed responsibilities."],["Does finishing an educational seven-step assignment make a person a CFP professional?","No. CFP certification and applicable registration require independent education, examination, ethics, experience and licensing criteria."]]},{"title":"Discovery is a source-controlled inventory of the household","body":["Collect cash and bank balances, retirement account statements, wages and other income, expense ledgers, credit balances, mortgage estimates, current insurance declarations, beneficiary forms, trust/deed records, family caregiving needs, tax returns and obligations. Each item needs date, source, ownership, status and limitations. Mark unknown or client-estimated values explicitly. A household can present $10,000 of savings while part belongs to a tenant deposit, unpaid business sales tax or pending customer refunds, making that amount ineligible for personal housing decisions.","Qualitative discovery is equally important: who depends on the earners, which spouse wishes to remain in a particular home, whether a child requires long-term support, how comfortably the clients experience volatility, and what tradeoffs they reject. Ask what success would look like in one, five and twenty years and how much flexibility remains. Informed consent to share personal or business details should be limited to the scope needed.","Use a source matrix distinguishing official statements from unsupported speculation. Actual posted wages and account balances override stale planning estimates, but future income remains uncertain. Avoid collecting credentials, Social Security numbers, minors' complete details or other secrets solely for a classroom case; the planning process can function using anonymized figures."],"worked":["Client estimates bank cash $12,000; reconciled statements show $10,000 accessible.","Of that $10,000, $3,000 is earmarked for overdue obligations, leaving $7,000 discretionary cash.","Retirement account $30,000 is not equivalent to $30,000 unrestricted checking cash.","The inventory should show account owner, restrictions, data date and confidence for all three."],"practice":[["Why distinguish a verified account statement from a client-recalled number?","Unknown or outdated figures can change solvency, liquidity and eligibility, so decisions need provenance and accuracy bounds."],["Why can a large retirement account not always cover immediate closing expenses without consequences?","Withdrawal rules, income tax, penalties, market changes and account restrictions may reduce accessible value or make it an unsuitable funding source."]]},{"title":"Goal selection converts wishes into constraints and dates","body":["Goals compete for the same limited dollars. A household may want to buy a home, fund retirement, protect a child, avoid late rent and improve credit. Each requires a target, horizon, minimum priority, consequence of failure and known funding sources. Distinguish mandatory goals—paying rent and maintaining insurance—from flexible preferences such as maximizing long-run nominal growth. Goals without a date or estimate cannot be objectively evaluated.","Suppose a household takes home $48,000 annually, spends $42,000 on baseline needs, and has a $6,000 annual cash margin. That is $500 monthly or 12.5% of net income; it is not automatically investable. If the household must build a $6,000 emergency fund within 24 months, depositing $250 monthly uses half the margin. Any remaining $250 must be reconciled with debt, mortgage cash, retirement and insurance obligations.","Write goal records with acceptance criteria: 'have $10,000 in accessible reserves by June 2028', 'reduce credit card balance below $1,000 by December', or 'prepare verified down-payment/closing funds before lender preapproval'. Distinguish aspirations from commitments and prioritize based on cash and legal reality."],"worked":["Annual net income $48,000, essentials $42,000 => annual margin $6,000.","Margin ratio $6k/$48k=12.5% of net income.","Set aside $250 monthly for 24 months => $6,000 reserve contributions before yield.","Only $250 monthly remains for other goals from the stipulated $500 margin."],"practice":[["Why is a 12.5% surplus not automatically a recommendation to invest it in stocks?","The surplus may first have to fund near-term emergencies, debt obligations, mortgage cash or insurance before market exposure is appropriate."],["How does a measurable goal differ from 'build generational wealth'?","It defines amount, deadline, beneficiary, responsible account and completion criteria rather than only an aspiration."]]},{"title":"Analyze alternative courses of action on common assumptions","body":["Compare the status quo against at least two coherent alternatives, holding fixed comparable income, tax assumptions, household needs and time horizon. For a high-interest credit card, compare paying minimums, accelerating repayment, and balancing repayment with a small emergency reserve. For a home, compare purchasing now, delaying for a cash cushion, and choosing a lower price. For insurance, compare appropriate term coverage with permanent products using total premiums and guaranteed features.","An initial account balance of $7,000 at a hypothetical 22% annual interest rate can accrue roughly $1,540 in one year under a simple unchanged-balance illustration; actual revolving interest uses daily calculations and payments. A $350 monthly payment is a different debt trajectory from a $50 monthly payment. The analyst must show the card's actual APR, minimums and payment calendar rather than invent a payoff date from average expenses.","Opportunity cost requires equal cash budgets. A $300 premium that leaves only $50 of a $350 monthly surplus for emergency funds materially changes the home plan, even if the policy claims tax benefits. Do not assume a complex product creates capital; document every new liability and ongoing charge."],"worked":["Card balance $7,000 × illustrative unchanged-balance APR 22% = $1,540 one-year simple interest.","Alternative A pays $350 monthly; alternative B pays $150; precise amortization requires monthly compounding and scheduled payments.","A $300 monthly premium uses 85.7% of a $350 monthly surplus.","Comparing only potential policy cash value to a brokerage portfolio without premium difference is invalid."],"practice":[["Why must planning alternatives use equal economic budgets?","A high-premium product must be compared against what the client could accomplish using the same available cash elsewhere."],["What makes a payoff estimate unreliable even if the current APR is known?","Missing payment timing, compounding conventions, fees, minimum payment rules and future changes can materially change payoff."]]},{"title":"Develop a balance sheet and cash-flow decision system","body":["The balance sheet records accessible cash, investable assets, home or business values, debt and restricted financial rights as of a stated date. Cash flow records inflows and outflows over a period. Solvency and liquidity can diverge sharply: the client can have positive retirement assets or home equity but not enough cash to pay a contractor. A comprehensive plan uses both, plus an income-tax and risk-protection layer.","Suppose liquid cash $10,000, retirement $30,000, vehicle $9,000 and liabilities $7,000 credit card plus $4,000 vehicle debt. Net worth is $38,000 under these simplified assumptions. Yet with $3,500 essential monthly outflows, the unrestricted $10,000 provides fewer than three months' expenses, and still less if credit payments or upcoming home purchases require cash.","Define a safe reserve based on verified essential expenses and genuine liquid access, not total net worth. Asset owners and beneficiaries matter: a joint bank account, an individually held IRA and a policy owned by an irrevocable trust cannot simply be treated as one fungible pool. Model financial independence using real inflation assumptions and after-tax withdrawals."],"worked":["Assets $10k cash+$30k retirement+$9k vehicle=$49,000.","Liabilities $7k card+$4k car loan=$11,000.","Net worth $49k−$11k=$38,000.","Essential monthly spending $3,500 means $10k liquidity covers ≈2.86 months before restrictions."],"practice":[["Can a $38,000 positive net worth guarantee an emergency expense is affordable?","No. Asset liquidity, timing, taxes, market risk and ownership restrictions determine what cash is available."],["Why must an IRA owned by one spouse be distinguished from a joint checking account?","Different ownership and tax rules determine current access, beneficiary rights and transfer outcomes."]]},{"title":"Recommendations require tax, insurance, mortgage and estate coordination","body":["A unified recommendation must show how a housing purchase changes spending, insurable income needs, taxable deductions, disability exposure, retirement contributions and later beneficiary decisions. A mortgage preapproval is not evidence a client can safely maintain the home after repairs or a job loss. Likewise, buying life insurance is not a substitute for cash reserves or a legally valid beneficiary plan.","Suppose a $250,000 house has a hypothetical 5% down payment of $12,500, closing costs $7,500 and necessary initial reserves $6,000. The upfront funding target is $26,000 before program-specific items. A buyer with $10,000 truly accessible cash falls $16,000 short; unknown assistance programs cannot be entered as certain funding. Mortgage insurance, property tax and maintenance require separate ongoing projections.","If the goal includes passing property to a child, request the actual recorded deed, existing mortgage, beneficiary forms and likely probate/tax obligations. A trust instrument alone may not transfer a home; an IRA and insurance policy have separate transfer mechanisms. Coordinate a state-licensed lawyer and tax preparer for issues outside the planner's competence."],"worked":["Home price $250,000 ×5% down=$12,500.","Closing expense $7,500 plus post-purchase reserve $6,000.","Total target $26,000; accessible $10,000 cash implies $16,000 short.","Mortgage funding needs to be recomputed if lender fees or assistance eligibility differ."],"practice":[["Why can a preapproved mortgage still be an unsuitable financial plan?","Approval may not reflect future repairs, cash reserves, property costs, family goals or an adverse income shock."],["Why should a financial planner avoid claiming a trust automatically transfers all the family's accounts?","Actual title, beneficiary paperwork, account-specific tax and transfer rules govern what the trust can control."]]},{"title":"Implementation ownership, milestones and independent verification","body":["A recommendation is not yet an executed outcome. Assign each action to the client, adviser, attorney, tax professional, insurer or mortgage lender, with deadline, required documents, evidence of completion, and dependencies. Saving for a down payment depends on a verified cash plan; changing account beneficiaries requires provider-confirmed updates; funding a trust-held home may need a properly recorded deed. The order matters.","Plan near-term essential actions first: stop further high-rate debt growth, secure overdue bills and emergency liquidity, obtain actual employer match and loan terms, verify protection gaps, and then compare investable assets. A planner should not tell a client to surrender an old insurance contract until replacement underwriting and claims protection are confirmed. Avoid putting a family at risk for the convenience of closing an implementation ticket.","A professional action register includes status proposed/authorized/submitted/confirmed, verification method and next review date. Wealth Lab academic exercises should not silently write to the user's progress or bank data when the user changes demonstration inputs; the only durable record of a real-world action is documentary evidence from the external responsible party."],"worked":["Goal 'obtain term quote' remains proposed when client requests options.","After client authorization and insurer issue/confirmation, actual coverage may be marked active.","An estate deed is not funded merely because a draft trust was downloaded.","If home cash goal is $16k short and monthly accumulation $400, simple timeline ≥40 months ignoring interest or aid."],"practice":[["Why shouldn't a planner mark a life insurance application as completed coverage?","Underwriting and contractual issuance may not yet be effective, leaving a dangerous protection gap."],["Why is an action log stronger than an impressive final slide presentation?","It identifies dependencies, responsible parties, documentary proof and deadlines that convert analysis into implementation."]]},{"title":"Monitoring through triggers rather than routine product churn","body":["An annual review is useful but major life events may demand earlier updates. Triggers include job loss, marriage, divorce, child needs, mortgage refinance, insurance changes, retirement-plan options, beneficiary death, illness or a large shift in tax law. Market losses alone do not mandate trading; revisions should link to objective changes in cash need, risk capacity or an agreed asset allocation.","Model plan performance against goal metrics: months of expenses funded, card principal paid, down-payment cash on hand, protection coverage confirmed, retirement contributions, actual property payment quotes, and heir administration documents. For a 6-month reserve target with $3,500 monthly essentials, the target is $21,000; $10,000 cash represents 2.86 months and a $11,000 gap before other obligations.","A monitoring memo states what evidence is needed, who supplies it, and what decisions will be reconsidered. Sensitivity ranges and uncertainty should not be hidden by precise-looking charts. If the professional did not agree to monitoring, the plan should explicitly identify what the client must revisit or assign to another qualified party."],"worked":["Six-month essential reserve target at $3,500/month is $21,000.","Accessible cash $10,000; remaining reserve gap $11,000.","At $500/month dedicated savings, simple gap timeline 22 months, ignoring earned interest.","If household experiences job loss, affordability and prioritization must be reassessed earlier than annual review."],"practice":[["Why may an immediate plan review be needed after job loss rather than at a scheduled annual date?","Risk capacity, monthly surplus, insurance eligibility and near-term cash needs can change abruptly."],["Does market volatility by itself prove the plan should trade its portfolio constantly?","No. Decisions should reflect objectives, cash need, risk tolerance and agreed monitoring policy, with taxes and turnover costs considered."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFP Board seven-step practice standards clarification","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/compliance-resources/2020/01/financial-planning-and-application-of-the-practice-standards-for-the-financial-planning-process"],["CFP Board Code and Standards client information","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/code-of-ethics-and-standards-of-conduct"],["SEC standards of conduct for account recommendations","https://www.sec.gov/about/divisions-offices/division-trading-markets/broker-dealers/staff-bulletin-standards-conduct-broker-dealers-investment-advisers-account-recommendations-retail"],["CFPB mortgage loan estimate and cash-to-close","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB budget and debt payment education","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-cards/answers/key-terms/"],["IRS retirement account withdrawal and rollover guidance","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"],["Connecticut Probate Court estate and trust resources","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates"],["Investor.gov investment risk and planning","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products"]],"assessment":{"title":"Seven-step client financial planning process dossier","prompt":"Conduct fictional client discovery with annual net income $48k, essential outflow $42k and $10k accessible cash; prioritize a six-month reserve against $3,500 monthly essentials, $7k card at 22% APR and a $250k potential house with 5% down, $7,500 closing costs and $6k post-purchase reserves. Evaluate original status quo, faster debt reduction, delayed home purchase, appropriate term coverage vs permanent borrowing. Build a balance sheet with $30k retirement, $9k vehicle and $4k vehicle loan. Document seven CFP Board steps without claiming professional certification, and give implementation roles, verification methods and monitoring triggers.","rubric":["Verified qualitative/quantitative discovery (20%)","Correct cash, debt and mortgage arithmetic (25%)","Alternatives, risk and client preference reconciliation (25%)","Implementation responsibility and professional referrals (15%)","Monitoring triggers and source quality (15%)"],"answerKey":["Annual cash margin $6k/$48k=12.5%, or $500 monthly before prioritization.","Assets $49k less liabilities $11k gives $38k net worth, but $10k cash covers only 2.86 months of $3.5k essentials.","Six-month reserve target $21k, current $10k means $11k additional liquidity needed.","Home cash 5%×$250k=$12.5k down+$7.5k costs+$6k post-close reserve=$26k, $16k above available $10k.","The seven steps are understand, goals, analyze, develop, present, implement, monitor/update with scope and responsibility documented.","A 22% high-interest credit card must be considered against safe reserve building and investing; do not automatically risk home cash in insurance or equities."]}},{"id":"L16.S3","title":"Regulation and Adviser Due Diligence","subtitle":"SEC and Connecticut oversight, Form ADV/CRS, broker verification, examinations, credentials and regulatory limits","prerequisites":["L16.S1 fiduciary standards and conflicts","L16.S2 client engagement and documented recommendations","L11.S1 tax claims and verification","L15.S3 financial marketing and leverage risks"],"objectives":["Differentiate adviser firm registration, broker-dealer registration, individual registration, Series 65 exam and CFP designation","Verify professionals and firms using Investor.gov IAPD, FINRA BrokerCheck and Connecticut Department of Banking","Read Form ADV Part 1, 2 and Form CRS for actual services, fees, conflicts and discipline","Explain the scope of SEC Reg BI and investment-adviser fiduciary duties","Identify red flags in insurance, investment, trust and tax marketing","Compare an account's disclosure and client documentation before funding it","Understand why academic study alone does not authorize regulated professional activity"],"sections":[{"title":"Know who regulates a professional and in what capacity","body":["The SEC regulates federally registered investment advisers under the Investment Advisers Act, while states supervise many smaller advisers and registered investment adviser representatives depending on jurisdiction and statutory exemptions. Broker-dealers and their registered representatives operate within a separate securities framework, including FINRA oversight for member firms and SEC Regulation Best Interest for covered retail recommendations. An individual might appear in multiple systems because of dual registration.","The Connecticut Department of Banking publishes state registration verification resources for investment advisers, adviser agents, broker-dealers and securities agents. An actual registration record should be checked for the legal entity and representative rather than assuming a familiar business name belongs to the particular person offering advice. An adviser licensed in another state is not automatically authorized for every service in Connecticut.","Professional planning credentials, degree programs, association memberships and qualifying examinations each have their own function. They can evidence knowledge but do not substitute for legally required firm or representative registration. Build a source-controlled status table with the regulator, professional, firm, registration or certificate number, date checked and restrictions."],"worked":["Example firm A appears on IAPD as SEC-registered adviser, with separately registered representative data.","Example B is a FINRA-registered broker; BrokerCheck is appropriate for its professional background.","Example C describes itself as a financial coach without stated securities registration; determine activities before inferring whether registration is required.","Formal exam credentials alone are not verified current registration or authorization."],"practice":[["Why should a client's due diligence verify both the firm and the individual?","A firm's registration does not by itself establish an individual representative's current status, employer, discipline or authorized capacities."],["Can a state-regulated investment adviser be assumed to have no fiduciary duty merely because it is not SEC registered?","No. Investment adviser obligations can apply under relevant state statutes and law; determine the correct framework and services rather than inferring from federal status alone."]]},{"title":"Investment Adviser Public Disclosure is an evidentiary starting point","body":["Investor.gov links to the Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD) system, which provides information on SEC and state-registered adviser firms and many adviser representatives. The firm Form ADV materials disclose business characteristics, services, fees, conflicts, affiliations, disciplinary events and other material information. The existence of a registration should not be misrepresented as government endorsement of the strategy or a guarantee that the firm is solvent.","Form ADV Part 1 contains registration and business information; Part 2A is a narrative firm brochure and Part 2B can provide brochure supplement details about supervised persons. Certain retail professionals and firms also deliver Form CRS relationship summaries with concise comparisons and discussion prompts. A client should examine current records, dates, amendments and supplementary evidence as appropriate.","For a hypothetical adviser charging an AUM fee, request the ADV stated rate, billing schedule, custody arrangement, investment management authority, conflicts and whether account transaction and underlying fund costs are separate. Read the actual narrative details rather than relying only on a one-page marketing pitch. Save a dated citation to official documents without copying private client credentials."],"worked":["Initial managed assets $200,000, ADV states 1% AUM advisory fee.","Opening annual approximate adviser fee $2,000 if held constant, before account costs.","Form ADV narrative may separately disclose fund expenses, commissions and conflicts.","Public registration is evidence of disclosed status, not a guarantee against future market loss."],"practice":[["Why is IAPD more useful than merely reading a firm website?","It provides official filings, status and discipline-related information to compare with promotional representations."],["Does Form ADV eliminate the need to ask about actual all-in household costs?","No. Fees can include underlying fund expenses and other charges, and current signed documents should corroborate what applies to the specific account."]]},{"title":"BrokerCheck and complaint histories require nuanced reading","body":["FINRA BrokerCheck allows consumers to review broker-dealer firms and registered brokers, including employment history, licenses, qualifying exams and public disciplinary or customer-dispute disclosures. A disclosure event warrants careful reading of dates, allegations, disposition and relevance; it is neither automatic proof of misconduct nor proof that the issue is immaterial. Absence of a recorded complaint also does not prove a product recommendation is safe.","A detailed due-diligence note records the exact professional name, CRD identifier, current association, registered capacities, state licenses and public disclosures. Search both the person and the relevant legal firm. Where a record is confusing, seek confirmation from the regulator or firm compliance department. This is a verification task, not a social media review contest.","A professional's compensation can still create a conflict when disclosure history is clean. Account recommendations should be analyzed by costs, features, reasonable alternatives and client goals. Never equate a clean BrokerCheck screenshot with a guarantee that the investment will earn the advertised yield."],"worked":["Adviser claims current state securities authorization, verified status must match relevant date and capacity.","BrokerCheck history shows a customer dispute labeled pending; allegation does not automatically equal final disciplinary finding.","The analyst records source, date, result and unresolved significance before a recommendation.","Verification conclusion can be 'needs further review', not only approve/reject."],"practice":[["Does one complaint automatically prove an investment professional violated securities law?","No. Review the nature and outcome of each public disclosure and distinguish allegations from findings or settlements."],["Does a clean professional background guarantee the securities product is suitable?","No. Suitability and best-interest analysis still depend on product risk, costs, client profile and available alternatives."]]},{"title":"Series 65 and Series 66 are examinations, not investment licenses","body":["NASAA explains that passing the Series 65 examination is normally an examination prerequisite for investment adviser representative registration, but passage by itself is not a license. An individual must satisfy applicable state registration processes and other requirements through the relevant firm. Some jurisdictions accept designated alternatives under current rules, subject to state confirmation. The examination is not evidence the candidate may immediately solicit or charge for regulated advisory services.","Series 66 is a combined exam that generally works alongside a valid Series 7 for the applicable representative qualification. Examinations and credentials may expire or have waivers depending on registration history and state-specific requirements. It is inaccurate to claim that earning an educational finance degree, completing a simulation, or reading a tax manual confers these permissions.","A CFP® certification is a professional credential subject to separate CFP Board requirements and continued ethical standards, not a replacement for necessary regulator registration when providing regulated securities advice. The student should be able to explain these distinctions to a hypothetical client without either discrediting legitimate professional education or overstating a certificate's legal scope."],"worked":["Hypothetical student passes Series 65 but has not joined a registered adviser and has not completed state registration.","Educational exam result exists; professional registration status may remain 'not registered'.","A public marketing claim 'licensed fiduciary because Series 65 passed' is unsupported.","Check current Connecticut requirements and NASAA FAQ before making a profession-specific recommendation."],"practice":[["Does passing Series 65 automatically allow a person to operate an investment-advisory business in Connecticut?","No. Licensing and registration involve applicable state or federal requirements beyond passing the exam."],["Is CFP certification the same as Securities and Exchange Commission registration?","No. CFP Board administers a private professional certification; securities adviser and broker registrations are separate regulatory matters."]]},{"title":"Reg BI and fiduciary disclosures in an actual account recommendation","body":["Regulation Best Interest imposes a best-interest standard when broker-dealers make covered securities recommendations to retail customers, together with disclosure, care, conflict and compliance obligations. The SEC investment adviser fiduciary interpretation focuses on duties of care and loyalty that apply to advisory engagements. A dually registered professional must make clear which capacity applies to a particular recommendation and cannot rely on ambiguous titles to blur role boundaries.","Consider a broker recommending a $100,000 fee-based advisory account over an available transaction brokerage account. If client trading is minimal, a recurring fee could cost more without enough additional service to justify it; if ongoing planning and monitoring are genuinely required, advisory fees may be reasonable. Proper analysis needs the client's trading, goals, access, cost comparison and services. Neither choice is automatically correct.","Form CRS provides a concise relationship overview, but other disclosures and individualized account agreements may contain essential details. A plan that recommends a rollover, annuity or high-commission policy should check applicable product regulation, penalties, guarantees and alternative approaches. Do not treat the same standards as automatically covering non-securities insurance and all estate services."],"worked":["Opening balance $100,000, hypothetical 1% annual advisory fee=$1,000.","Alternate transaction account could incur $100 annual trading costs under stipulated low-activity scenario.","Starting annual dollar difference $900 before differing service value, fund charges or custody fees.","The appropriate recommendation needs scope and reasonable alternative analysis."],"practice":[["Does Reg BI always apply to every conversation about a will or insurance policy?","No. It addresses covered broker-dealer securities recommendations; insurance and estate services can fall under different applicable laws and professional standards."],["Why should dual-registration capacity be disclosed when an account is recommended?","The services, costs, standards, conflicts and legal obligations can depend on whether the professional is acting as an adviser or broker."]]},{"title":"Detect materially misleading investment and insurance claims","body":["Red flags include promises of guaranteed high returns without a credible guarantor, tax-free borrowing without loan interest or lapse discussion, 'no downside' asset protection from an LLC or trust, private investments sold without verifiable underlying assets, and pressure to act before documents can be reviewed. Strong due diligence follows source records, the contract, actual economic cash flows, fair comparison and client capacity for losses.","An investment advertised at 12% annual return and financed by a personally guaranteed 10% loan is not a guaranteed 2% arbitrage. Fees, income taxation, asset price risk, maturity mismatches and creditor enforcement can make the expected spread negative. A permanent policy funded with $400 monthly premiums can also deplete near-term savings before it builds meaningful accessible surrender value. These claims require contract illustrations and adverse cash-flow modeling.","Lawful tax deductions and estate designs do exist. The analyst must avoid rejecting them categorically while insisting on proper client facts and qualified tax/estate professional verification. Ethics means withholding endorsement when material numbers or authorization cannot be established, rather than claiming the strategy must be fraudulent or must be safe."],"worked":["Loan $50,000×10% annual simple cost=$5,000.","Promoted expected 12% project return=$6,000, just $1,000 gross spread before taxes and fees.","Stress project −20% produces −$10,000 investment loss plus $5,000 financing expense.","The lender's claim persists regardless of the projected return."],"practice":[["Why is a guaranteed loan obligation combined with nonguaranteed investment return not riskless?","The investor may receive no return or suffer losses while fixed loan payments and collateral requirements continue."],["What should an analyst do when an adviser refuses to provide a current contract or audited figures?","Mark the claim unverified and avoid recommending a commitment until appropriate evidence is obtained."]]},{"title":"Connecticut registration, complaints and escalation channels","body":["Connecticut's Department of Banking offers a license verification portal covering investment advisers, investment adviser agents, brokers and other regulated financial service categories. Its Securities and Business Investments Division oversees relevant state activities and maintains consumer protection resources. If a person asserts a license or an advisory firm exists in Connecticut, verify official current status and the exact named entity before sending money.","A consumer can also use Investor.gov and FINRA BrokerCheck to check federal and securities industry filings. When a concern exists, identify the proper regulator and gather complete factual records: advisor name, signed agreement, promotional representation, dates, account statements and communications. Report concerns through the regulator's official channels rather than sharing sensitive files publicly.","Professional boundaries remain: an accountant may compute taxes but not necessarily draft trust deeds or give securities advice without required authorization; an estate lawyer may draft trusts but need separate authority to manage investments. A collaborative planning team should define roles, confidentiality permissions, and responsibility for maintaining records."],"worked":["An investment representative claims licensure in Connecticut but provides only an unrelated brokerage company's marketing webpage.","Verification status remains unknown until official Connecticut or federal/FINRA records match the person and firm.","A client should preserve date and documentation of the representation.","No client funds should be sent based solely on an unverified professional title."],"practice":[["What is an appropriate Connecticut-specific first step when confirming an investment adviser's status?","Use the Connecticut Department of Banking verification resources and IAPD, matching exact firm/person details and date."],["Can an insurance agent, broker, CPA and estate attorney be assumed to hold identical legal authority?","No. Each profession and regulated activity has different licensing, competence and professional practice boundaries."]]},{"title":"A formal professional verification and disclosure audit","body":["Design an audit with registration, credentials, firm identity, current status, compensation, Form ADV Part 2, Form CRS when applicable, BrokerCheck disclosure history, written account fees, proposed holdings, rollover consequences, conflicts, termination charges and signed scope of engagement. Every fact should have evidence and a date. Do not invent IDs or assume disciplinary details for a person who has not been identified.","Score whether a professional has made sufficient evidence available, but do not force a simple score to override serious problems. An inappropriate recommendation or an unresolvable material conflict may make the engagement unsuitable even when all paperwork is filed. State what remedies would address unknown facts and which professional or regulator should be consulted.","Conclude with a client-facing summary using plain language: the service you will receive, cost, legal role, how recommendations are selected, potential incentives and what might happen during a downturn. The responsible professional should be able to explain the economics without jargon or unsupported guarantees."],"worked":["Opening portfolio $200,000, 1% adviser plus .5% funds => $3,000 modeled annual opening cost.","Client can obtain Form ADV and applicable Form CRS to ask about scope and conflicts.","Adviser background checked using actual IAPD and, as applicable, BrokerCheck records.","Review remains conditional until all material asset/cost and professional status evidence is current."],"practice":[["Why is a checklist marked 'all documents received' insufficient by itself to certify advice is appropriate?","Documents may disclose serious conflicts or unsuitable costs; professional judgment still must analyze the client-specific consequences."],["How should the audit present incomplete registrations or unavailable disclosures?","Use a clearly documented unresolved status and specify official sources or questions needed to verify the missing facts."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Connecticut Department of Banking verify a license portal","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/about-dob/index-pages/verify-a-license"],["CT adviser registration details and IAPD linking","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/securities-licensing/licensing-general/see-if-an-investment-adviser-is-registered"],["SEC Investor.gov IAPD adviser records","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/using-iapd"],["Investor.gov FINRA BrokerCheck verification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/using-brokercheck"],["NASAA examination and licensing FAQ","https://www.nasaa.org/exams/exam-faqs/"],["SEC Regulation Best Interest compliance guide","https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/small-business-compliance-guides/regulation-best-interest"],["SEC Form CRS filing and delivery FAQ","https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/trading-markets-frequently-asked-questions/frequently-asked-questions-form-crs"],["CFP Board financial planning Code of Ethics","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/code-of-ethics-and-standards-of-conduct"]],"assessment":{"title":"Investment professional verification and regulatory disclosure examination","prompt":"Conduct a fictional due-diligence review for an adviser who proposes moving $100k to a recurring 1% advisory account from a $100 annual-transaction-fee brokerage relationship. Explain the conditions that trigger broker-dealer Reg BI versus investment adviser fiduciary standards, CFP Board duties, and professional license verification using IAPD, BrokerCheck and Connecticut DOB. Identify Form ADV Parts 1/2 and Form CRS, disciplinary data caveats, conflict, monitoring and rollover boundaries. Analyze a 12%-project-financed-by-10%-loan pitch on $50k with −20% stress. Distinguish Series 65 passing from registration. Write an auditable client-facing verification memorandum.","rubric":["Accurate regulatory framework and scope (25%)","Registration and official document verification (25%)","Fee and loan opportunity-cost analysis (20%)","Claims, complaints and conflicts nuance (20%)","Reporting and privacy ethics (10%)"],"answerKey":["1% of $100k=$1,000 annual advisory opening fee versus stipulated $100 brokerage trades, a $900 cost difference before service value.","Reg BI covers specified broker-dealer retail securities recommendations with four obligations, while investment-adviser fiduciary care/loyalty applies to adviser engagements; CFP Board standards apply to CFP professional financial advice.","IAPD, BrokerCheck and CT DOB should be used for official dated professional status and disclosure records. Passing Series 65 does not itself create registration.","At $50k, 12% hypothetical return $6k, 10% loan simple interest $5k => only $1k optimistic spread. A −20% investment year loses $10k plus $5k interest.","The audit should distinguish complaint allegations from disciplinary findings and registration from endorsement or performance guarantee."]}},{"id":"L16.S4","title":"Comprehensive Financial Plan Capstone","subtitle":"Five-year Connecticut household strategy, debt elimination, mortgage affordability, stress testing, protection and generational wealth","prerequisites":["M1–M5 income, cash flow, budgeting and debt","M6–M7 mortgage and financial mathematics","M8–M12 investments, retirement and taxes","M13–M15 protection, estate, rental and business strategy","L16.S1–S3 fiduciary, planning process and official verification"],"objectives":["Construct an integrated financial statement and reconcile all goal funding to available cash","Model fixed-rate mortgage payments and stress home carrying costs","Compare high-interest debt reduction against investing with liquidity guardrails","Measure emergency reserves and job-loss cash burn","Estimate life/disability insurance needs without substituting permanent policy values for cash","Build a realistic five-year timeline with no double-counting of home savings","Distinguish estate/beneficiary ownership from generational housing cash support","Deliver a properly cited five-year professional financial planning memorandum"],"sections":[{"title":"The master case: a household with competing financial goals","body":["The fictional Connecticut household consists of two adults and one intended child beneficiary. Annual take-home income is $65,000, annual core spending $48,000, unrestricted cash savings $10,000 and credit card debt $7,000 with a hypothetical 22% APR. The household hopes to buy a $250,000 home while protecting emergency liquidity, investing for retirement and eventually passing property or financial assets to the child. Existing coverage, retirement balances, loan eligibility and current mortgage offers are explicitly unknown until verified.","The initial annual cash margin is $65,000−$48,000=$17,000, or about $1,416.67 monthly. But this amount must cover debt repayment, savings, potential insurance premiums, taxes not already reflected, transition costs and any changed housing expenses. The model must not count the same cash for a reserve, mortgage down payment, policy premium and retirement contribution. Each goal needs a separate ledger and timing.","This is a five-year planning problem, not an invitation to generate a single 'best investment' answer. A competent solution presents the present position, the required target cash, alternative repayment pathways, resilience under adversity, dependencies on external lending and legal rules, and a prioritized implementation sequence."],"worked":["Net annual income $65,000; core spending $48,000 => margin $17,000/year.","Monthly income $5,416.67, monthly core spend $4,000, preliminary surplus $1,416.67.","Liquid unrestricted cash $10,000; revolving card $7,000 at stated 22% APR.","Near-term home and reserve allocation cannot assume existing $10k funds are both spent and retained."],"practice":[["What is the first error in treating $17,000 of annual cash margin as guaranteed investment money?","Debt, reserves, insurance, cash-to-close and changing ownership expenses may all require some of the surplus; future income and spending can change."],["Why must the current household facts be labeled fictional and unverified?","Real borrowing and legal recommendations require actual statements, lender terms, ownership records and current client authorization."]]},{"title":"Balance sheet, liquidity and economic solvency","body":["A planning balance sheet separates cash, retirement assets, vehicle/home and other marketable property, debts and contractual protections. The master case gives $10,000 liquid unrestricted assets and $7,000 of known revolving liabilities, yielding $3,000 simplified known net worth before unreported property and debt. The planner must not assert that the household's full net worth equals this partial figure. Unknown vehicles, retirement accounts and business interests should be marked missing rather than assumed zero.","At $48,000 yearly core spending, essential monthly spending is $4,000 under the case assumptions. Ten thousand dollars covers 2.5 months without another income source. A three-month reserve target is $12,000, creating a $2,000 gap; six months requires $24,000, creating a $14,000 gap. If card repayment immediately consumes all cash, the household loses a buffer and could return to expensive borrowing at the first emergency.","Cash allocation must consider credit risk and reserve quality. Available credit-card limits are not cash reserves, life policy projected surrender value is not guaranteed liquidity, and home equity without existing ownership is irrelevant. Use separate short-term money accounts and document why any debt payoff is staged instead of immediate."],"worked":["Known liquid cash $10,000−known credit-card liability $7,000=$3,000 PARTIAL known balance-sheet net amount.","Essential spending $48,000/12=$4,000/month.","3-month reserve target $12,000, cash shortfall $2,000.","6-month reserve target $24,000, cash shortfall $14,000."],"practice":[["Does the $3,000 partial net amount prove the family's complete net worth?","No. The case lacks verified retirement, vehicle, other liabilities and assets, which must be collected before a full statement is possible."],["Why might immediately using every dollar of savings to pay the card be risky?","It leaves the household unable to fund unpredictable essentials and can trigger more high-rate debt despite saving some interest."]]},{"title":"Credit card payoff: amortization, interest and cash-priority rules","body":["High-rate revolving debt deserves rigorous attention. A simplified monthly amortization uses opening balance B, monthly rate r=APR/12, payment M and formula B_n=B(1+r)^n−M[(1+r)^n−1]/r while balance remains positive. This approximation differs from real credit-card average daily balances, billing cycles, fees, variable rates and payment timing. Use actual bank statements and the exact account agreement in practice.","With $7,000 at 22% nominal APR and a fixed $500 monthly end payment, the modeled card is paid in approximately sixteen monthly payments, with the final payment smaller than $500. A $350 monthly payment produces a longer schedule and more total interest. Paying $7,000 outright from the $10,000 cash reserve could save future interest but leave only $3,000—or 0.75 months of $4,000 essential expenses—before rebuilding savings.","A suitable plan can protect a minimum emergency amount while applying a predictable share of monthly surplus to debt. Determine if debt balances grow, whether other higher-rate obligations exist, whether refinancing really reduces all-in cost, and whether late fees are already owed. State the choice, the household's liquidity floor and a trigger to accelerate payments when reserves are met."],"worked":["Opening revolving balance $7,000, nominal 22% yearly rate approximated as .22/12 monthly.","With modeled $500 monthly payments, payoff requires about 17 payment dates depending on final-payment convention; compute iteratively.","Immediate full $7,000 card payoff leaves $3,000 cash from $10,000, only .75 months of expenses.","Payoff analysis must include remaining cash and real terms, not only modeled interest savings."],"practice":[["Does paying a 22% card instantly with all available cash always maximize safety?","It reduces interest but can destroy needed liquidity; compare emergencies and the risk of needing additional borrowing."],["Why should a card payoff lab compute final partial payment instead of assuming equal full payments forever?","Debt reaches zero during a final billing period, so continuing to charge fixed full payments would overstate cost or imply negative balance."]]},{"title":"Home affordability needs both closing cash and monthly resilience","body":["A home purchase has initial down payment, closing costs and cash reserve obligations, followed by mortgage principal and interest, municipal tax, insurance, possible mortgage insurance, utilities, maintenance and repairs. A lender's preapproval is not a household resilience certificate. With a hypothetical $250,000 home, 5% down is $12,500 and the financed principal before loan expenses is $237,500. Assumed closing costs $7,500 and a $6,000 post-close emergency reserve imply a $26,000 initial total cash target.","At a hypothetical fixed 6.5% nominal rate on $237,500 for 30 years, the principal-and-interest payment follows the amortizing formula; exact taxes, homeowner insurance, PMI and other housing costs must be added separately. The present household already spends $48,000 annually on core items, which may include rent and thus cannot be combined blindly with the projected full home cost. Remove replaced rent before adding a new mortgage and owner expenses.","Price, down-payment assistance and lender terms must be verified; Connecticut and federal programs can have income, occupancy, property or repayment restrictions. Never treat prospective grants as committed resources or policy loans as guaranteed qualifying down payment. If the house requires a $9,000 roof in year one, the post-closing $6,000 reserve may be inadequate."],"worked":["5%×$250,000 = $12,500 down, loan principal $237,500.","Plus $7,500 assumed closing and $6,000 post-purchase reserve = $26,000 target.","Hypothetical monthly P&I for $237,500 at 6.5% over 360 months ≈$1,501.","Property tax, insurance, PMI, maintenance and old rent replacement require additional independent calculations."],"practice":[["Why must a housing affordability model remove existing rent before adding the new full home cost?","Otherwise it double-counts an expense that will cease after closing, overstating costs and distorting the monthly margin."],["Does $1,501 mortgage principal and interest equal the full cost of owning a $250,000 house?","No. Property tax, insurance, mortgage insurance, repairs, utilities, possible HOA and transaction effects can materially increase outflow."]]},{"title":"Three scenarios: base plan, job disruption and property surprise","body":["Prepare base, employment disruption and housing surprise cases. The base case assumes income and baseline spending remain as provided and assigns monthly surplus to protected reserves, debt elimination and a future down payment. A job-loss case removes one or both paychecks for a defined duration while essential bills continue. The property surprise adds a major repair or insurance premium jump after closing. Do not assume emergencies are independent of adverse markets or household health.","Suppose the household has $10,000 unrestricted cash and all $4,000 core monthly spending continues during two months with no income. The cash burn is $8,000, leaving $2,000 before additional irregular costs. If the household paid the card in full before job loss and held only $3,000, two months of essential spending creates a $5,000 shortfall absent other resources. This illustrates why debt and emergency allocations cannot be optimized independently.","A $9,000 roof bill shortly after closing can also exceed the stipulated $6,000 post-closing reserve by $3,000, before other bills. Review homeowner insurance exclusions and deductibles; ordinary wear and roof end-of-life may not be insured losses. A resilient plan records where the additional $3,000 would actually come from or delays the purchase until reserves improve."],"worked":["Two-month essential spending at $4,000/month=$8,000.","With $10,000 liquid cash and no income, remaining balance $2,000 before other costs.","If card was paid off using $7,000, remaining $3,000 cash means $5,000 short after two zero-income months.","Roof $9,000 vs $6,000 post-closing reserve gives a $3,000 gap."],"practice":[["Why is a no-income scenario useful even if the household currently feels secure?","A finite cash burn shows whether the plan can survive a realistic interruption without relying on volatile assets or expensive credit."],["Why should a planner stress home repairs separately from an insurance claim?","Deductibles, exclusions and ordinary wear often leave owner cash responsibilities regardless of insured coverage."]]},{"title":"Insurance, retirement, taxes and inheritance must fit together","body":["The household's protection plan should size death benefit against dependents, debts, future support and genuine resources, then consider disability income and employment coverage. An agent's proposal of high-premium permanent life insurance is not automatically suitable while cash reserves and high-rate debt are underfunded. Compare affordable term coverage, employer benefits and permanent policy characteristics using written prices and guarantees. Insurance cash value is not an unlimited untaxed pool.","Retirement saving may have an employer match and tax advantages, but the exact plan must be identified. Funding all retirement accounts while missing card minimums or home cash can be imprudent; skipping a significant employer match may also sacrifice valuable compensation. Evaluate accessible emergency cash, sustainable monthly contributions, time horizon and tax eligibility. Do not model tax deductions as full cash rebates.","For the intended child heir, map actual title, mortgage, primary and contingent beneficiaries, IRA income tax consequences, and who can administer the home if either adult dies or becomes disabled. A revocable trust must be correctly funded where used; an irrevocable policy trust can limit owner access. A future heir needs enough cash for property tax, maintenance and utility bills, not simply an ownership document."],"worked":["Hypothetical $7,000 card at 22% could cost $1,540 simple annual interest at unchanged balance.","Permanent premium offer $300/month consumes $3,600/year of cash margin.","The household's annual margin is $17,000; multiple commitments must sum to ≤available sustainable cash.","An heir inheriting $250,000 house with large debt receives home equity, not an unencumbered $250,000 cash gift."],"practice":[["Why should retirement, insurance and debt be reviewed as a coordinated cash plan?","They compete for identical monthly cash and carry differing tax, risk and liquidity consequences; optimizing one in isolation may hurt others."],["Does adding a child's name as a future trust beneficiary solve property maintenance funding?","No. Deed ownership, creditor claims, taxes, mortgage payments and continuing property cash must be managed separately."]]},{"title":"Five-year funding roadmap with verifiable milestones","body":["A five-year roadmap starts with essential bills and a protected minimum emergency floor, then systematic card payoff and steady down-payment accumulation. The order depends on verified monthly cash, current obligations and risk profile. Do not promise a home acquisition in a specified month unless target cash, mortgage qualification and ongoing affordability are satisfied. Down-payment assistance applications and tax planning may be useful but remain conditional until written approval.","At a hypothetical $500 monthly directed to the card, the debt is modeled paid in roughly sixteen to seventeen months depending on exact terms. The freed $500 can then be directed to home cash or emergency funds. Starting from $10,000 liquid, a $26,000 housing cash goal is a $16,000 initial gap if all existing cash were devoted to that goal, but that would leave no independent emergency reserve beyond the stipulated post-closing $6,000. Keep the cash definitions consistent before selecting a target.","Use implementation milestones such as verified card balance below $1,000, emergency cash ≥three months essentials, cash-to-close and post-close reserve independently funded, loan estimate verified, policy coverage confirmed, beneficiary forms updated and annual portfolio review completed. Assign client, lender, accountant, insurer or attorney to each task with evidence and a planned review trigger."],"worked":["A five-year calendar spans 60 monthly periods.","$500 monthly directed toward the card from current $1,416.67 preliminary margin uses about 35.3% of that margin.","Known housing cash target $26,000, current unrestricted cash $10,000 creates at least $16,000 difference before clarifying separate emergency cash.","Savings milestone records must distinguish amounts already allocated to closing versus emergency buffers."],"practice":[["Why must an emergency reserve remain separate from cash-to-close?","Closing consumes down-payment and transaction money, while emergencies still require accessible cash immediately afterward and before closing."],["Why is a target purchase date conditional?","Actual credit score, income, mortgage terms, taxes, property condition, program eligibility and down payment must be confirmed."]]},{"title":"Final defense: two recommendations and a peer-review audit","body":["Create an integrated planning memorandum with a client-facing version and a technical appendix. The technical version contains equations, dated inputs, alternative cases, risk bounds and legal/tax source citations. The client-facing version communicates three most important actions, reasons, tradeoffs and possible failure modes in plain language. A strong plan does not drown the client in all sixteen modules; it selects what is material to the household.","An independent peer reviewer should reproduce the credit-card payoff, amortizing mortgage payment, emergency-months calculation, income gap, portfolio stress, fees, and potential estate financing needs. Any discrepancy must be resolved with an updated calculation and transparent revision note. A spreadsheet labeled 'approved' without lender, insurer or legal confirmation remains an educational scenario, not a completed transaction.","Present two defensible options—for example buy later with stronger reserves, or pursue a cheaper home after debt payoff—and state which conditions could reverse the recommendation. The capstone should teach the boundary between actionable financial education and regulated client-specific professional work: exams and self-study do not make the student a licensed adviser or estate attorney."],"worked":["Known net annual income $65,000, baseline core outflow $48,000 => $17,000 margin.","Mortgage illustration $237,500 over 360 at 6.5% requires verified monthly principal-and-interest calculation.","Debt $7,000 at 22%, emergency spending $4,000/month and cash $10,000 create competing priorities.","No claim of guaranteed assistance, guaranteed market returns or completed trust/insurance transfers without documentation."],"practice":[["What makes a final capstone defensible rather than a collection of calculators?","It reconciles all goals to one cash budget, tests correlated downside, cites verified documents, compares feasible alternatives and assigns accountability."],["What must be true before a student calls the hypothetical plan implemented?","External actions require actual client approvals, documented account/contract changes and confirmations from the responsible lender, insurer or legal professional."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFP Board seven-step process standards","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/compliance-resources/2020/01/financial-planning-and-application-of-the-practice-standards-for-the-financial-planning-process"],["SEC investment adviser best-interest account recommendation guidance","https://www.sec.gov/about/divisions-offices/division-trading-markets/broker-dealers/staff-bulletin-standards-conduct-broker-dealers-investment-advisers-account-recommendations-retail"],["CFPB Mortgage Loan Estimate official guide","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB credit card interest education","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-cards/answers/key-terms/"],["IRS 2026 retirement tax and withdrawal guidance","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans"],["NAIC insurance contract illustration principles","https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/life-insurance-illustrations"],["Connecticut Probate Courts trust and estate administration","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates"],["Connecticut Department of Banking verify professional licensing","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/about-dob/index-pages/verify-a-license"],["Investor.gov risk and diversification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products"]],"assessment":{"title":"Complete five-year household financial-plan portfolio defense","prompt":"As lead analyst, produce a 1,500–2,500 word integrated five-year recommendation for fictional Connecticut family with net pay $65k, core expenses $48k, cash $10k, card $7k at 22%, a child beneficiary, possible $250k home and unknown insurance and retirement details. Build a 60-month cash schedule, compute cash and reserve ratios, card payoff with $500 monthly, 5% down and $7,500 costs/$6,000 post-close reserve, 30-year P&I on $237,500 at assumed 6.5%, two-month job disruption and $9k repair. Compare now-vs-later home purchase, insurance term vs policy borrowing, employer match, investment account risk, and will vs properly funded trust with beneficiaries. Provide verified vs unknown source table, professional referral roles, and monitoring triggers. Do not assert degree or license from this course.","rubric":["Complete balance sheet and one consistent cash budget (25%)","Debt, mortgage and income-shock numerical accuracy (25%)","Insurance, retirement, tax and estate integration (20%)","Feasible alternatives and professional conduct (20%)","Implementation and evidence/monitoring plan (10%)"],"answerKey":["Annual surplus $17,000 and monthly initial margin $1,416.67, before goal earmarks.","$10k accessible cash/$4k monthly essential costs=2.5 months; 3-month goal $12k, 6-month $24k.","Known cash less card $7k = $3k PARTIAL known net amount, not complete household net worth.","5%×$250k=$12.5k down, +$7.5k costs+$6k post-close reserves=$26k, compared with only $10k available cash.","Amortizing $237.5k at 6.5% nominal APR over 360 months yields ~$1,501 monthly principal and interest; taxes, insurance, PMI, maintenance excluded.","Two-month loss of all income costs $8k essentials; $10k cash leaves $2k, while cash after immediate $7k card payoff would be $5k short.","$9k roof minus $6k assumed post-closing reserve leaves $3k funding gap.","All cash and legal actions remain conditional on lender, insurer, state-specific estate lawyer, retirement plan and other verified records."]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Module 16 — Professional Financial Planning Qualification-Style Integrated Final","intro":"This is a rigorous educational assessment of analytical planning skills, not a CFP credential, state license, university accreditation or individual financial advice. Use the same one-year figures and real cash ledger across all steps. Carefully distinguish verified sources, unknown facts and hypothetical interest/return assumptions. Every conclusion must be appropriate to the client's goals and safety constraints.","scenario":["Client case: two adults, one intended minor child beneficiary, Connecticut residency, household annual after-tax take-home $65,000, annual core spending $48,000, unrestricted checking/savings $10,000. Explicitly no confirmed retirement or home property values yet.","Credit card: $7,000 current balance at a hypothetical fixed 22% APR; compare $350 and $500 fixed monthly payments under r=APR/12 and exact final payment. Real card minimums and billing rates differ.","Home objective: hypothetical $250,000 Connecticut primary residence, 5% down, $7,500 closing fees, $6,000 protected after-purchase reserve, illustrative fixed 6.5% note rate and 360 monthly payments, no lender preapproval yet.","Mortgage ongoing owner expenses: use separately stipulated annual property tax $4,200, homeowners insurance $1,200, maintenance reserve $3,000, PMI $100 monthly and utilities $2,400 annually for the exam only; do not assume these are local quotes.","Cash allocation: an emergency reserve must remain truly accessible; starting core spending $4,000/month. Verify that pre-purchase spending includes rent, which must be subtracted before adding the new owner costs.","Employment stress: no household earnings for two months, with $4,000/month essential expense baseline, no assumed unemployment benefits and no immediate sale of stocks.","Property surprise: $9,000 urgent roof replacement after closing; typical wear or deferred maintenance may not qualify as insured losses.","Insurance proposal: a seller suggests a $300/month permanent life policy and promises future loans for home cash, but only the seller's projected non-guaranteed numbers are available. Alternative term coverage price and actual insurable need are not yet known.","Retirement plan status unknown. Request employer contribution/match, expenses and distribution rules; do not invent tax deduction or matching deposits.","Property succession desired to child; no verified deed, named beneficiaries, executed/funded trust, or executor documents. Mortgage remains payable by heirs or estate.","Advisor remuneration comparison: $200,000 hypothetical portfolio, advisory AUM rate 1%, fund expenses 0.5%; employer retirement plan expense 0.15% and proposed IRA total 1.3% on separate $100,000 example.","Professional verification: check adviser via SEC Investor.gov IAPD, FINRA BrokerCheck if applicable and Connecticut DOB. Interpret adviser fiduciary, broker Reg BI and CFP Code separately.","Ethics: salesperson paid a commission for permanent insurance, conflict/fee disclosure required under applicable standards. No legitimate policy can guarantee borrower net worth rises through the loan.","Plan timeline: five years, review at milestones and after job loss, new dependents, retirement changes, mortgage changes or death. Stage obligations from household income, not borrowed available credit.","Every numerical result has reproducible formulas and declared timing; avoid rounding earlier intermediate calculations or silently changing hypothetical assumptions."],"tasks":["Establish signed engagement scope, capacity, confidentiality and what client authorizations and records remain missing.","Prepare an asset/liability and cash-flow source-control matrix with data quality, date and ownership.","Calculate annual and monthly take-home cash margin, and ratio of surplus to income.","Compute accessible emergency months for $10k cash and $4k essential expenses, and shortfalls to 3-month and 6-month targets.","Build a repayment schedule for $7k card at 22% APR and $500 monthly until exact final payment; compare $350/month alternative.","Explain why paying entire $7k card from $10k cash creates a liquidity risk despite avoiding interest.","Compute gross required home cash: 5%×$250k, stipulated $7.5k closing and $6k post-closing buffer; explain double counting.","Compute fully amortizing 30-year monthly mortgage P&I from $237.5k at 6.5%.","Compute stated ongoing property costs separately and compare to replaced rent; do NOT simply add housing total to core expenses.","Stress two months of no income with $4k monthly essential spending and differing starting reserves.","Stress post-closing $9k roof against $6k assumed reserve and propose a financing-free response.","Conduct insurance-needs fact discovery and compare affordable term with high-premium permanent loan pitch, including conflicts.","Calculate initial layered adviser fees on $200k, and 20-year illustrative fee compounding effect at 6% gross and simplified 4.5% after-fee rate.","Compare $100k employer plan 0.15% versus advisory IRA 1.3%, plus services, taxes, protections and conflicts.","Distinguish SEC adviser fiduciary, broker-dealer Reg BI and CFP Board financial advice standards.","Verify registration and disclosures with official IAPD, BrokerCheck and Connecticut Department of Banking, explaining Series 65 limits.","Create a retirement and after-tax asset allocation plan conditioned on verified employer match, risk capacity and liquidity.","Create a legally appropriate ownership/beneficiary/trust verification checklist, incorporating mortgage debt and child support funding.","Create base, job-loss and property-expense stress case outputs with feasible recommended actions and tradeoffs.","Present a 1,500–2,500 word client memo with a technical appendix, quantitative worksheets, source citations, task owners, trigger review dates and professional ethics review."],"answers":["Annual margin $65k−$48k=$17k; monthly margin $17k/12≈$1,416.67, ratio $17k/$65k≈26.15% of take-home.","Liquid reserve $10k/$4k monthly=2.5 months; three months $12k needs $2k, six months $24k needs $14k.","At immediate full card repayment, cash $3k and emergency coverage .75 months; at full income interruption two months require $8k, cash shortage $5k.","Mortgage cash required $250k×.05=$12,500 down +$7,500 closing+$6,000 protected post-close = $26,000, initial cash $10,000 means $16,000 not-yet-funded difference BEFORE separating other emergency goals.","Principal of 5%-down home $237,500, 360 months r=.065/12; P&I =P*r/[1−(1+r)^−360] ≈ $1,501.16/month; exact code calculation preferred.","Stipulated annual property taxes $4,200 + insurance $1,200 + repairs reserve $3,000 + PMI $1,200 + utility $2,400 = $12,000/year or $1,000/month in addition to note P&I, subject to comparing discontinued rent and changes.","Cash $10k after two-month $4k/month essentials = $2k; with $3k cash after immediate card payoff = −$5k deficit absent support.","Roof $9k versus planned $6k post-close reserve => $3k insufficient, before other expenses. Homeowners insurance may not cover ordinary roof aging.","1% advisory +.5% underlying fund on $200k=$3,000 opening annual fees; fee-compounded 20-year illustrative no-fee $200k×1.06^20 vs simplified net $200k×1.045^20 differs substantially.","$100k at .15% annual plan expense costs $150; at 1.3% proposed IRA $1,300; opening annual cost difference $1,150, before service, legal protections and tax distinctions.","Identify adviser fiduciary duty of care and loyalty, broker Reg BI four pillars, CFP Board fiduciary duty for financial advice; do not conflate professional capacity.","Series 65 exam passage is not itself state registration, CFP credential is not a license, and official status should be verified at IAPD, BrokerCheck and CT DOB.","Death/disability insurance coverage, homeowner funding, retirement match and trust transfers are all unknown until actual documents, quotes or insurer/lender/lawyer confirmation.","Any life policy loan is liability-bearing and can accrue interest, reduce net death benefit and trigger lapse taxation; projected savings are not guaranteed.","Correct recommendation preserves accessible emergency reserves, pays high-cost debt according to an affordable schedule, accumulates real closing funds and defers unsupported loan/inheritance claims."],"rubric":["Client discovery, ethics, authority, registration and records (15%)","Numerical household budgeting, debt, mortgage and emergency analysis (30%)","Alternatives: insurance, investment, taxes and homeownership (25%)","Intergenerational protection, legally valid ownership and risk (15%)","Implementation, monitoring and clear client presentation (15%)"],"sources":["https://www.cfp.net/ethics/compliance-resources/2020/01/financial-planning-and-application-of-the-practice-standards-for-the-financial-planning-process","https://www.cfp.net/ethics/code-of-ethics-and-standards-of-conduct","https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/small-business-compliance-guides/regulation-best-interest","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/using-iapd","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/using-brokercheck","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/about-dob/index-pages/verify-a-license","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/","https://www.nasaa.org/exams/exam-faqs/","https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/life-insurance-illustrations","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates"]}};
